Regarding the presentation delivered by Orlan and Adrian, the initiative focuses on standardizing GitHub Copilot usage across our test automation engineering teams.

As part of the GitHub Copilot rollout, we identified inconsistencies in how automation engineers were using the tool. To address this, Orlan and Adrian developed a reusable framework and practical playbook that can be shared and adopted across teams.

Key highlights:

- Standardized approach: Established a centralized ".github" framework containing common instructions, reusable prompts, and skills to guide consistent GitHub Copilot usage.
- Core automation scenarios: Focused on Selenium UI automation, REST Assured API testing, Java coding standards, code refactoring, flaky test stabilization, and failed-test analysis.
- Practical guidance: Mapped common automation activities to recommended Copilot prompts and skills, including required inputs and validation steps.
- Improved consistency and quality: Introduced common automation standards and coding practices to improve code quality, maintainability, and alignment across teams.
- Faster adoption and onboarding: Created a reusable knowledge base that helps engineers leverage GitHub Copilot more effectively and reduces individual experimentation.
- Scalability: Established a foundation that can be shared across our automation engineering community and progressively extended with additional use cases and best practices.

Overall, the objective is to move from individual, inconsistent usage of GitHub Copilot toward a standardized, scalable approach that improves engineering productivity and automation quality.

